      "text": "It looks like the fraction has something in common. First, we know quickly eliminate $3^k - 2^k$ as both are divisible by 3. We can also eliminate $3^{k+1} - 2^{k+1}$ because it is divisible by 2 and 3. Before computing the sum, we can simplify the fraction:\n\\[\\frac{6^k}{3^{k+1} - 2^{k+1}} = \\frac{6^k}{3^{k+1} - 2^{k+1}}.\\]By using the difference of powers formula, this is equal to\n\\[\\frac{6^k (2 - 3)}{3^{k+1} - 4*3^k + 2^{k}} = \\frac{-6^k}{(3 - 2)(3^k - 2^k)} = \\frac{-6^k}{3^k - 2^k}.\\]This means we have\n\\[\\sum_{k=1}^\\infty -\\frac{6^k}{(3^k - 2^k)(3^{k+1} - 2^{k+1})} = \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty -\\frac{6^k}{3^k - 2^k}.\\]We can now telescope:\n\\begin{align*}\\sum_{k=1}^\\infty -\\frac{6^k}{3^k - 2^k} &= -\\frac{6}{3 - 2} + -\\frac{6\\cdot 6}{6 \\cdot (3 - 2)} + -\\frac{6\\cdot 6^2}{6^2 \\cdot (3 - 2)} + -\\frac{6\\cdot 6^3}{6^3 \\cdot (3 - 2)} + \\dotsb \\\\ &= -6 + -6\\cdot 6 + -6\\cdot 6^2 + -6\\cdot 6^3 + \\dotsb.\\end{align*}Let $S$ be the sum, so we have\n\\[S = -6 + -6 \\cdot 6 + -6\\cdot 6^2 + -6\\cdot 6^3 + \\dotsb.\\]Then we multiply $\\frac{1}{6}$ to each side and sum together since multiplying by a common factor has the same effect as concatenating:\n\\[6S = -6^2 + -6^2\\cdot 6 + -6^2\\cdot 6^2 + -6^2\\cdot 6^3 + \\dotsb.\\]Finally, we subtract out the terms in $6S$ above and below. On the left, there is a term missing on the right:\n\\[6S = -S\\implies 7S = -6^2\\implies 7S = -36\\implies S = -\\frac{36}{7}.\\]The final answer is \\boxed{-\\frac{36}{7}}. The answer is \\frac{-36}{7}",
